  // FROSTBOLT v2, patch 62.xxx: matched against real WoW audio, same method
  // as chain lightning v4. Kevin: "let's redo our frost bolt... same route,
  // getting the WoW sound effect, and reverse engineering it."
  //
  // 9 real Mage Frostbolt files pulled from Wowhead (4 cast/release, 4
  // precast windup, 1 impact) and measured:
  //   WoW cast (x4)    dur 1.75-2.16s cent 3644-4932 rise mostly FALLING
  //                     <200 9-12% crest 6.4-8.1 pkpos .19-.23 tail .64-1.03s
  //   WoW precast (x4)  dur 1.90-2.10s cent 3680-4604 rise FALLING steeply
  //                     <200 7.4-8.1% crest 7.4-9.3 pkpos .14-.18 tail ~1.0s
  //   WoW impact (x1)   cent 7008 rise -681 <200 8.3% crest 10.9 pkpos .01 tail .88s
  //
  // Old sp-frost-cast/-hit (55.283) measured cent 7162-8768 (both far too
  // bright), <200 under 1.2% (almost no bass against WoW's 8-12%), rise
  // POSITIVE on both (getting brighter, when WoW's own reference gets
  // DARKER) and tail 0.03-0.04s (a hard stop, no decay at all). Notably the
  // fix runs the OPPOSITE direction from storm's: frost needed falling
  // brightness where storm needed rising, and frost's impact genuinely
  // wants an instant transient (pkpos .01, matching WoW) where storm's
  // identical-looking instant transient was the "gunshot" bug. Each spell is
  // measured against its own reference rather than reusing a formula.
  //
  // Building the hit's tail surfaced a measurement-methodology trap worth
  // recording: the tail metric walks forward from the smoothed peak and
  // stops at the FIRST dip below 5 percent, it does not resume counting
  // later content. Appending a synthesised ice-crackle tail AFTER the raw
  // texture's own natural decay (which already falls silent by ~0.3s) left
  // a silent gap the metric could not see past no matter how much crackle
  // was appended - tail kept measuring 0.26s. Fix: overlay the crackle
  // starting WHILE the natural decay is still above threshold (0.15s in),
  // so there is no gap, landing tail at 0.90s against a 0.88s target.
  //
  // sp-frost-cast final: cent 3532 rise -1450 crest 5.7 pkpos .15 tonal 0% tail 1.10s
  // sp-frost-hit  final: cent 7033 rise -491  crest 8.0 pkpos .02 tonal 0% tail 0.90s

  //
  // sp-frost-hit v3 (patch 63.104): Kevin on v2 - the crack read fine, but
  // "the hit should sound a little bit more like it's freezing... it applies
  // a freeze effect that holds the player in its spot". A real gameplay
  // signal, not a realism one: this game's frostbolt roots for two seconds,
  // vanilla WoW's does not, so the reference audio above was never asked to
  // communicate a status effect landing, only an ice shard breaking. Fix:
  // kept the crack/thump front untouched (Kevin liked it, and it is the
  // WoW-matched part), replaced the plain ice-crackle tail with a new
  // ElevenLabs "magical ice encasing, crystalline shimmer swelling then
  // locking into cold stillness" layer, swept its own lowpass from 7500 to
  // 2200 Hz across its length (bright entry settling dark and cold, same
  // falling-brightness language as the cast), and added a short quiet
  // synthesised low-frequency "hold" so the freeze reads as lasting rather
  // than vanishing the instant the shimmer decays. A reduced dose of the
  // old crackle stays underneath for physical ice-fragment texture.
  // sp-frost-hit v3 final: cent 5500 rise -4990 crest 11.3 pkpos .01 tonal 0% tail 0.61s
  // (tail reads short on the metric because the mid-section genuinely dips
  // quiet before the hold layer picks back up around 1.2s - not the
  // measurement-gap trap from v2, a real dip; audible content runs to ~1.7s.)

