  // FOOTSTEPS (patch 68.x). Kevin's brief named WoW as the reference. Real
  // WoW terrain footstep files (Wowhead's Footsteps category,
  // MON_Footstep_Bipedal_Foot_<size>_<material>) turned out to be ONE POOL
  // of one-shot variants PER MATERIAL with no separate walk/run recordings
  // at all - WoW differentiates gait by animation cadence and impact force,
  // not by swapping samples. That finding reshaped this set: one texture
  // per material (wood, dirt, sand, metal), three variants each for the
  // same no-immediate-repeat rotation chop/mine already use, with walk vs
  // run handled at PLAYBACK time (gain/pitch, see footTick_ in the patch)
  // rather than as separate generated takes.
  //
  // Shaped against real WoW reference numbers (measure.py, /tmp/footref):
  //   wood  target cent 1000-1500, <200Hz .48-.52 (a hollow plank
  //         resonance), instant peak, short .13-.14s tail. Shipped set:
  //         cent 1619-1877 (a little bright), <200Hz .65-.70 (correct
  //         direction, high bass reads as hollow), pkpos .06 (matches).
  //   dirt  target cent 1900-2500, <200Hz .17-.30, no ring. Shipped:
  //         cent 1651-1791 (a touch under), <200Hz .22-.25 (in range).
  //   sand  target cent 3970-4240 (brightest material), <200Hz near zero,
  //         longest tail. Shipped: cent 3993-4332 (matches), pkpos .06-.07
  //         (matches, after reshaping the amplitude envelope forward - the
  //         raw ElevenLabs "sinking into sand" takes swelled LATE, peaking
  //         past the middle of the clip, which read as a whoosh rather
  //         than a footstep until an exponential front-decay was applied).
  //   metal target cent 1240-1750 (a dull plate clank, NOT a bright ring -
  //         footfall force is far below a weapon strike, so this is a
  //         different target than combat-hit-plate at cent ~4947). Shipped:
  //         cent 2043-2270 (a bit bright), tail .12-.13 (matches exactly).
  // Every material differs from ElevenLabs' raw output the same direction
  // every foley sound in this track has: too bright, not enough low end.
  // Fixed the same way as every other patch here: a lowpass sweep sized to
  // the material's own reference measurement, plus a short bandpassed-noise
  // "body" layer (NOT a pure sine - an early wood/metal attempt used a sine
  // sub and it read as tonal content measure.py flagged at 21-50 percent,
  // a footstep-scale echo of the frost/storm "pitched voice is not free"
  // lesson: at this short a clip, a pure tone is proportionally loud enough
  // to be heard as a note, not a body).

